\begin{align*}
\frac{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} + \sqrt{x^2 + z^2} + \sqrt{y^2 + z^2}}{x + y + z} &= \frac{(x^2 + y^2) + (x^2 + z^2) + (y^2 + z^2) + 2\sqrt{x^2y^2 + x^2z^2 + y^2z^2}}{(x + y + z)^2}.
\end{align*}
By the "alternate means inequality," we must have
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{x^2y^2 + x^2z^2 + y^2z^2} &\le xy + xz + yz.
\end{align*}
After multiplying both sides by $2,$ and noting that the denominators are the same, we obtain
\begin{align*}
\frac{(x^2 + y^2) + (x^2 + z^2) + (y^2 + z^2) + 2\sqrt{x^2y^2 + x^2z^2 + y^2z^2}}{(x + y + z)^2} &\le \frac{(x^2 + y^2) + (x^2 + z^2) + (y^2 + z^2) + 2(xy + xz + yz)}{(x + y + z)^2}.
\end{align*}
Expanding the numerator on the RHS, pulling the common factor out, and then canceling one $x + y + z,$ we obtain
\begin{align*}
\frac{(x^2 + y^2) + (x^2 + z^2) + (y^2 + z^2) + 2(xy + xz + yz)}{(x + y + z)^2} &= \frac{[(x + y)^2 + (x + z)^2 + (y + z)^2] - 2(xy + xz + yz)}{(x + y + z)^2}\\
&= \frac{(x + y)^2 + (x + z)^2 + (y + z)^2 - 2(xy + xz + yz)}{(x + y + z)^2}\\
&= \frac{x + y + x + z + y + z}{x + y + z}\\
&= 2.
\end{align*}
From this reasoning, we see that, by the alternate means inequality,
\[ \frac{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} + \sqrt{x^2 + z^2} + \sqrt{y^2 + z^2}}{x + y + z} \ge 2, \]
and it is clear that equality occurs not only when $x = y = z,$ but also when $x, y, z$ contain equality or inequality in some specific way.
Thus, the minimum value is $\boxed{2}$ and the minimum is reached when $x = y = z.$</answer>

